        <!-- Content -->
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
            <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

                <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
                    Most deck stain failures aren't stain failures at all — they're cleaning failures wearing a stain's name. Stain applied over dirt, mildew, or sun-bleached wood fibers sits on top of a compromised surface instead of bonding into it, and it peels, flakes, or blotches within a single season. Cleaning is not a courtesy step before staining. It is the step that determines whether the stain job survives its first winter.
                </p>

                <h2 id="why-cleaning-before-staining-actually" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Why Cleaning Before Staining Actually Matters
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Wood stain — whether penetrating oil or film-forming acrylic — needs open, absorbent wood fiber to bond to. Three things routinely block that bond, and all three are invisible or easy to dismiss until the stain job fails:
                </p>
                <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-700">
                    <li><strong>Surface contamination.</strong> Dirt, pollen, tannin bleed, and general grime sit on top of the wood grain and physically prevent stain from penetrating. This is the layer most homeowners think a garden hose removes. It usually doesn't.</li>
                    <li><strong>Biological growth.</strong> Mold, mildew, and algae don't just look bad — they're living organisms with root structures (hyphae) that penetrate into the wood cells. A pressure washer blasts away the visible surface growth and leaves the roots intact. Stain applied over those roots seals in an active mold colony that will bloom right back through the finish.</li>
                    <li><strong>UV-damaged, oxidized fiber.</strong> Gray, weathered wood isn't dirty in the traditional sense — the wood's lignin (the compound that binds cell fibers together and gives wood its color) has been broken down by UV radiation. That degraded gray layer is dead, loosely bound fiber that won't hold a stain film no matter how well it's cleaned. It has to be chemically reversed or removed, not just washed.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    Stain applied over any of these three conditions looks fine on application day. The failure shows up 3 to 12 months later as peeling, flaking, blotchy absorption, or a stain job that simply never developed the water-beading protection it was supposed to. If you're shopping for a finish, see our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">best deck stain reviews</a> for what to put on the wood once it's actually ready. And if your deck is pressure-treated lumber, read <a href="/can-you-stain-pressure-treated-wood/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">can you stain pressure-treated wood</a> before you start — timing matters as much as cleaning does on new PT decking.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The good news: cleaning is cheap, fast relative to the payoff, and forgiving of DIY execution as long as you match the right cleaner chemistry to the actual problem on your deck. That match is the entire point of this guide.
                </p>

                <h2 id="by-problem-type" class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Which Deck Cleaner Do You Need? Match by Problem
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Not every deck has the same problem, and using the wrong cleaner chemistry wastes a weekend without fixing anything. Identify what's actually going on with your wood, then jump to the matching product category below.
                </p>

                <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
                    <div class="border-l-4 border-blue-500 bg-blue-50 p-5 rounded-r-xl">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 mt-0">Routine Dirt, Pollen &amp; Grime (Deck Looks Basically Fine)</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Use: <strong>an all-purpose oxygen bleach or sodium percarbonate deck cleaner.</strong> This is the standard, default pre-stain wash for a deck that isn't badly weathered or moldy — just dirty from a season of use. It's the right starting point for most annual maintenance and most restaining jobs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="border-l-4 border-gray-500 bg-gray-100 p-5 rounded-r-xl">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 mt-0">Gray, Sun-Bleached, Weathered Wood</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Use: <strong>an oxalic-acid-based wood brightener.</strong> Oxalic acid is the only chemistry that chemically reverses UV graying by dissolving the iron-tannin compounds that cause the discoloration and reopening the wood's pH so stain can bond again. A standard cleaner will not fix gray wood — it'll come out clean and still gray.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="border-l-4 border-green-600 bg-green-50 p-5 rounded-r-xl">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 mt-0">Mold, Mildew &amp; Algae (Green or Black Spotting)</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Use: <strong>a mildewcide-strength cleaner formulated to kill biological growth at the root,</strong> not just lift it off the surface. Shaded, humid, or north-facing decks need this category even if the wood underneath isn't otherwise weathered.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="border-l-4 border-yellow-500 bg-yellow-50 p-5 rounded-r-xl">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 mt-0">New or Recently Installed Pressure-Treated Decking</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Use: <strong>a mill-glaze-cutting cleaner</strong> before the first stain application. New PT lumber comes from the mill with a smooth, compressed "mill glaze" surface from the planing and treatment process that resists stain absorption. It needs to be chemically etched open, not just rinsed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="border-l-4 border-red-500 bg-red-50 p-5 rounded-r-xl">
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 mt-0">Old, Failing, or Peeling Stain</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Use: <strong>a dedicated stain stripper,</strong> not a cleaner. Cleaners remove dirt and biological growth from bare or lightly weathered wood; strippers chemically break down and lift an existing film-forming stain or the remnants of a peeling semi-solid or solid coat. Trying to clean your way through failing stain instead of stripping it produces an uneven, blotchy result underneath the new coat.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
