                                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Old, failing stain</td>
                                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dedicated stripper (not a cleaner)</td>
                                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cured film-forming coating that must be removed</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h2 id="top-picks" class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Best Deck Cleaners: Our Top Pick for Every Problem
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Five categories, five picks. Each one is our top recommendation for the specific problem it solves — not a generic top-10 list where half the entries don't apply to your deck.
                </p>

                <!-- 1. All-Purpose / Best Overall -->
                <div class="space-y-8 mt-10">
                    <div class="border-2 border-[#38BDF8] rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-md bg-white relative">
                        <div class="absolute -top-3 left-6 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-3 py-1 rounded-full">★ Best Overall</div>
                        <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-blue-600 mb-1 mt-2">Best All-Purpose Cleaner for Routine Pre-Stain Cleaning</p>
                        <h4 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2">DEFY Wood Cleaner</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            DEFY's all-purpose formula is a sodium-percarbonate-based (oxygen bleach) cleaner designed as the standard pre-stain wash for decks that are dirty but not severely weathered or moldy — which describes the majority of decks getting a routine annual or biennial restain. Sodium percarbonate breaks down into hydrogen peroxide and soda ash when mixed with water, releasing oxygen bubbles that lift dirt, pollen, tannin staining, and light surface grime out of the wood grain without attacking the wood fibers themselves the way chlorine bleach does.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            It's biodegradable and safe to use around landscaping, lawns, and garden beds bordering the deck — a real consideration since cleaning runoff inevitably ends up in the soil below. Mix per the label, apply with a pump sprayer, agitate with a stiff synthetic-bristle deck brush while the solution is still working, and rinse thoroughly with a garden hose or a pressure washer kept under 1,200 PSI with a wide fan tip. It won't reverse existing gray weathering and it isn't strong enough for established mold colonies — for those, jump to the categories below — but as the default "get the deck clean and ready" product before applying a fresh coat of stain, it's the one we reach for first.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl">
                            <ul class="m-0 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Oxygen-bleach chemistry is safe for all wood species and won't damage fibers, biodegradable and landscape-safe, effective on dirt, pollen, and tannin staining, no wood etching or fuzzing.</li>
                                <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Doesn't reverse gray UV weathering on its own, not strong enough for heavy mold or mildew infestations, requires reasonably warm water for full activation.</li>
                                <li><strong>Best For:</strong> Routine pre-stain cleaning on decks that are dirty but not badly weathered, moldy, or mill-glazed — the majority of annual and biennial restaining jobs.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- 2. Gray Weathered Wood -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm hover:shadow-lg transition-shadow bg-white">
                        <div class="inline-block bg-purple-100 text-purple-800 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-3">Best for Gray, Weathered Wood</div>
                        <h4 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">Wolman DeckBrite Wood Brightener</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            Gray, sun-bleached decking isn't dirty — it's chemically degraded. UV radiation breaks down lignin at the wood surface and reacts with naturally occurring tannins to form iron-tannate compounds, which is the actual chemistry behind that dull gray cast. No amount of scrubbing or oxygen bleach removes it, because it isn't sitting on the surface — it's a change to the wood fiber itself. Oxalic acid is the answer: it's a mild, naturally occurring acid (also found in rhubarb and spinach) that dissolves those iron-tannate compounds and restores the wood's pH to a range where stain can bond properly again.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            Wolman DeckBrite is applied as a second step after washing — clean first with an all-purpose or mildew cleaner to remove dirt and biological growth, rinse, let the deck dry slightly, then apply the brightener and watch the gray visibly lift within minutes as the wood's natural color returns. It needs a thorough water rinse afterward to neutralize the acid and stop the reaction; skipping this step can leave a chalky residue that interferes with stain adhesion. This is also the step that "opens" weathered wood grain back up for stain absorption, which is why brightening is treated as a distinct step from cleaning rather than a stronger version of the same thing.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl">
                            <ul class="m-0 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Only chemistry that genuinely reverses UV graying, restores natural wood pH for better stain bond, works fast (visible results in 10–15 minutes), safe on all wood species including cedar and redwood.</li>
                                <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Requires gloves and eye protection (it's a mild acid), needs a thorough rinse to avoid residue, doesn't kill mold or mildew on its own — clean for biology first.</li>
                                <li><strong>Best For:</strong> Decks with visible gray, sun-bleached, or weathered coloring that a standard cleaner leaves untouched — especially older decks going years between recoats.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- 3. Mold/Mildew -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm hover:shadow-lg transition-shadow bg-white">
                        <div class="inline-block bg-green-100 text-green-800 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-3">Best for Mold &amp; Mildew</div>
                        <h4 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">RESTORE Deck &amp; Fence Wash</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            Shaded decks, north-facing decks, and anything near mature trees or dense landscaping fight a losing battle against mold, mildew, and algae — organisms that don't just discolor the surface but send root structures (hyphae) into the wood cells themselves. RESTORE's Deck &amp; Fence Wash is formulated around a stronger oxidizing action specifically to disrupt that biological growth at the root, not just bleach the visible green or black spotting off the top layer. That distinction matters: a cleaner that only lifts surface discoloration will look great for a few weeks before the colony regrows from the roots it never touched, right through the new stain coat.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            Apply with a pump sprayer, allow a full dwell time (typically 10–15 minutes — check the label, and don't let it dry on the surface), then agitate heavily contaminated areas with a stiff brush before rinsing. For decks with established, dark mildew staining that's clearly been building for more than one season, a second application is common and worth budgeting time for rather than assuming one pass will finish the job.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl">
                            <ul class="m-0 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Kills mold and mildew at the root rather than just bleaching surface spotting, strong performance on shaded and humid decks, works on siding, fencing, and other exterior wood as well as decking.</li>
                                <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Stronger dwell time and rinse requirements than a standard cleaner, heavily contaminated decks often need a second application, less effective on gray UV weathering (use an oxalic brightener for that after).</li>
                                <li><strong>Best For:</strong> Decks with visible green algae or black mildew spotting, especially in shaded, humid, or poorly ventilated areas where growth returns quickly after a light cleaning.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- 4. New/Mill-Glazed -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm hover:shadow-lg transition-shadow bg-white">
                        <div class="inline-block bg-yellow-100 text-yellow-800 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-3">Best for New &amp; Mill-Glazed Wood</div>
                        <h4 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">Olympic Deck Cleaner</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            New pressure-treated lumber has a problem most first-time deck owners have never heard of: mill glaze. During milling, the high-speed planer blades that surface the boards compress and burnish the outermost wood fibers, creating a smooth, slightly glossy surface that resists stain absorption even after the lumber has fully dried and off-gassed its treatment moisture. A deck that "beads water" when it should be soaking it in almost always has mill glaze still intact, not a moisture problem.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            Olympic's oxygen-bleach deck cleaner is formulated to etch through that compressed surface layer rather than just wash the top of it, opening the grain enough for a penetrating stain to actually absorb. Apply generously, scrub with a stiff-bristle brush working across and with the grain, and rinse thoroughly. After cleaning, run the water-bead test described in our step-by-step section below — if water still beads instead of soaking in, the deck needs another pass or additional dwell time before staining. This step is also the reason we recommend waiting the standard 90–120 days after PT lumber installation: the wood needs to dry to below roughly 19% moisture content before mill-glaze cleaning and staining will actually take, which our <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-pressure-treated-wood/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">best deck stain for pressure-treated wood</a> guide covers in more depth.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl">
                            <ul class="m-0 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Etches through mill glaze that a standard rinse won't touch, oxygen-bleach base is safe on new, untreated wood fiber, widely available at big-box retailers, budget-friendly.</li>
                                <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Won't fix graying (not the problem new wood has), requires real scrub effort on heavily glazed boards, best paired with a water-bead test to confirm readiness rather than staining on a schedule alone.</li>
                                <li><strong>Best For:</strong> New or recently installed pressure-treated decking getting its first stain application, where water is beading on the surface instead of absorbing.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- 5. Stain Stripper -->
                    <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm hover:shadow-lg transition-shadow bg-white">
                        <div class="inline-block bg-red-100 text-red-800 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-3">Best Stain Stripper for Old, Failing Stain</div>
                        <h4 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-2">Wolman DeckStrip Stain &amp; Finish Stripper</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            When a deck has peeling, flaking, or heavily inconsistent old stain — semi-solid or solid coatings that have started to fail, or a mismatched history of different stain types layered over the years — cleaning alone can't fix it. A cleaner removes dirt and biology from bare or lightly weathered wood; it does not chemically break down a cured film-forming coating. Trying to clean your way through failing stain instead of stripping it leaves patchy remnants under the new coat, which telegraph straight through as an uneven, blotchy finish no matter how good the new stain is.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mb-4">
                            Wolman DeckStrip is a gel-consistency stripper that clings to vertical rail surfaces as well as horizontal deck boards, which matters because gravity pulls thinner strippers straight off railings before they've had time to work. Apply a heavy, even coat, let it dwell per the label (typically 15–30 minutes, longer in cool temperatures), and scrub with a stiff brush or use a pressure washer with a stripping tip to lift the softened coating off. Plan on this being a full-day project for anything larger than a small deck — stripping is the most labor-intensive step in deck restoration, and it's also the one that's most tempting to shortcut. Don't: any old stain left behind will block the new stain from bonding in exactly the spots you missed. After stripping, a wood brightener pass is usually needed to neutralize the stripper chemistry and restore an even color base before the new stain goes on — see our <a href="/deck-stain-colors-guide/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">deck stain colors guide</a> for choosing a finish once the wood is back to bare, even color.
                        </p>
                        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-xl">
                            <ul class="m-0 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
                                <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Gel formula clings to vertical surfaces and railings, effective on solid and semi-solid failed coatings, works on both oil- and water-based stain remnants.</li>
                                <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Labor-intensive, dwell and scrub time is significant on heavily coated decks, requires a follow-up brightener pass for even color, not needed (or appropriate) for bare or lightly weathered wood.</li>
                                <li><strong>Best For:</strong> Decks with old, peeling, or flaking stain — especially solid-color coatings — that need to come off completely before restaining.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Chlorine vs Oxygen Bleach -->
                <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-8 my-16">
                    <h2 id="why-we-don-t-recommend" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
                        Why We Don't Recommend Chlorine Bleach Deck Cleaners
                    </h2>
                    <p class="text-gray-700">
                        Straight chlorine bleach (sodium hypochlorite) is still sold as a "deck cleaner" solution in some old-school DIY advice and cheap big-box products, and it's still the wrong default choice for most decks. Here's why we steer people toward oxygen bleach instead:
                    </p>
                    <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-700 mt-4">
                        <li><strong>It damages wood fibers.</strong> Chlorine bleach is a strong oxidizer that breaks down lignin indiscriminately — not just surface contamination but the structural wood fiber underneath. Repeated chlorine cleaning over the life of a deck measurably weakens and roughens the surface grain, leaving fuzzy, degraded wood that absorbs stain unevenly.</li>
                        <li><strong>It doesn't reliably kill mold at the root.</strong> Chlorine bleach bleaches the visible pigment out of mold and mildew spores, which makes a colony look dead without actually killing the root structure embedded in the wood. The discoloration often returns within a season because the organism was cosmetically bleached, not eliminated.</li>
                        <li><strong>It can discolor and gray the wood.</strong> Chlorine reacts with wood tannins and can leave a washed-out, grayish cast that's difficult to correct — the opposite of what you want right before staining, and a problem an oxalic acid brightener then has to work around rather than simply reversing UV weathering.</li>
                        <li><strong>It's harsh on landscaping and metal hardware.</strong> Chlorine runoff kills grass and plants far more aggressively than oxygen bleach, and it accelerates corrosion on deck screws, joist hangers, and other fasteners over repeated use.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-4">
                        Sodium percarbonate (oxygen bleach) cleaners have become the standard recommendation across the deck-care industry for good reason: they lift dirt and light biological growth just as effectively for routine cleaning, without the fiber damage, without the same graying risk, and without the landscape toxicity. For established mold and mildew colonies, a dedicated mildewcide-strength cleaner formulated for root-level kill (like the RESTORE pick above) does the job that people used to reach for straight chlorine bleach to do — without the tradeoffs. Chlorine bleach isn't banned from deck care entirely, but for pre-stain cleaning specifically, oxygen bleach and purpose-built mildewcide cleaners are simply the better tool for the job.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- Step by step -->
                <div id="how-to-clean" class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-16 rounded-r-2xl">
                    <h2 id="how-to-clean-and-brighten" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
                        How to Clean and Brighten a Deck Before Staining: Step-by-Step
                    </h2>
                    <div class="space-y-5">
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 1: Clear and Protect the Area</h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Move furniture, planters, and grills off the deck. Wet down surrounding grass and garden beds with plain water before applying any cleaner — a soaked lawn dilutes and buffers any incidental overspray, and it's a five-minute step that prevents a much longer conversation with your landscaping afterward.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 2: Sweep and Pre-Rinse</h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Sweep off loose debris, leaves, and dirt. Pre-rinse the entire deck with plain water from a garden hose — this softens embedded grime and gives you a clean baseline to actually see what you're dealing with once it dries slightly.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 3: Apply the Cleaner and Let It Dwell</h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Mix your chosen cleaner per the label and apply with a pump sprayer, working in manageable sections so it doesn't dry on the surface before you can scrub it. Respect the dwell time on the label — usually 10–20 minutes depending on the product and the severity of what you're cleaning. Don't let it dry out; mist with water if it's evaporating too fast in direct sun or heat.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 4: Agitate with a Stiff Brush</h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Scrub with a stiff synthetic-bristle deck brush, working with the grain. This mechanical agitation is doing real work that the chemical alone won't — it's what actually lifts ground-in dirt and disrupts mold and mildew growth rather than just letting the cleaner sit on the surface.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 5: Rinse Thoroughly</h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Rinse completely with a garden hose or a pressure washer kept under roughly 1,200–1,500 PSI with a wide fan tip held at a consistent distance and angle. Higher pressure or a narrow tip held too close raises and damages wood fiber — you'll create a fuzzy, splintered surface that looks worse than the dirt you started with and absorbs stain unevenly.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 6: Brighten if the Wood Is Still Gray</h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">If the wood still looks gray or dull after it dries from cleaning, apply an oxalic-acid-based wood brightener as a separate step. Let it dwell per the label, watch the color visibly lift, then rinse thoroughly again to neutralize the acid and clear any residue.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 7: Run the Water-Bead Test Before Staining</h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">Once the deck is fully dry — typically 24–48 hours in warm, dry weather, longer in humid or cool conditions — sprinkle water on a few boards. If it soaks in within a minute or two, the wood is ready for stain. If it beads and rolls off, either the wood isn't fully dry yet, or the mill glaze / old finish wasn't fully removed. Don't stain over a failed bead test; it's the single fastest way to guarantee a callback for peeling stain a few months later.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 id="how-long-to-wait-between" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    How Long to Wait Between Cleaning and Staining
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Timing is where a lot of otherwise-careful DIYers lose the benefit of good cleaning work. The wood needs to be genuinely dry — not just dry to the touch on the surface — before stain goes on, because any residual moisture trapped in the grain will push the stain back out from underneath as it evaporates, leading to blotching and early peeling.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>In warm, dry, low-humidity weather,</strong> plan on 24–48 hours between the final rinse and staining. <strong>In humid climates or cooler weather,</strong> extend that to 3–4 days, and don't rely on a calendar guess — run the water-bead test from Step 7 above the morning you plan to stain. It takes thirty seconds and it's the only reliable confirmation that the wood is actually ready, as opposed to just looking dry.
                </p>
                <p>
                    New pressure-treated lumber is a special case: it needs to dry from its treatment-process moisture content, not just from the cleaning water, which is a much longer timeline — typically 90–120 days from installation. Cleaning new PT decking too early to "get ahead" of the project doesn't shorten that wait; the wood still needs to reach roughly 19% moisture content or below before stain will hold. For the full breakdown on when and how to stain PT lumber specifically, see <a href="/can-you-stain-pressure-treated-wood/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">can you stain pressure-treated wood</a> and our dedicated <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-pressure-treated-wood/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">best deck stain for pressure-treated wood</a> picks.
                </p>

                <h2 id="common-deck-cleaning-mistakes-that" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Common Deck Cleaning Mistakes That Ruin the Stain Job
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Most stain failures we hear about trace back to one of these mistakes happening weeks earlier during cleaning — long before the stain can was even opened.
                </p>
                <ul class="space-y-4 text-gray-700">
                    <li><strong>Pressure washing without a chemical cleaner first.</strong> High-PSI water alone can blast dirt off the surface, but it does nothing to kill mold roots or reverse graying, and used carelessly it raises and fuzzes the wood grain — creating a rougher surface that actually absorbs stain less evenly than a properly cleaned one.</li>
                    <li><strong>Staining on a fixed schedule instead of confirming with a water-bead test.</strong> "It's been 48 hours, it must be dry" is a guess. Humidity, shade, wood density, and cleaner residue all change the real drying timeline. The thirty-second bead test removes the guesswork.</li>
                    <li><strong>Skipping the brightener step on visibly gray wood.</strong> Cleaning and brightening are two different chemical jobs. A deck can be perfectly clean and still gray, and stain applied over that gray, degraded fiber layer looks dull and absorbs unevenly no matter how good the cleaning job was.</li>
                    <li><strong>Not rinsing cleaner or brightener residue completely.</strong> Leftover cleaner chemistry — especially oxalic acid residue — can interfere with stain adhesion and, in some cases, cause a chalky or hazy finish under certain stain formulas. A thorough final rinse is not optional.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cleaning in direct, hot sun.</strong> Cleaner solutions can dry on the surface before they've had time to work, and the same heat that dries cleaner too fast will dry the rinse water unevenly, leaving streaking. Work in the shade or during cooler parts of the day when possible.</li>
                    <li><strong>Using the wrong product for the actual problem.</strong> An all-purpose cleaner won't fix gray weathering. A cleaner won't strip failing stain. Matching the chemistry to the actual condition of the wood — not just grabbing whatever's on the shelf at the store — is the single biggest factor in whether cleaning day actually accomplishes anything.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="best-time-of-year-to" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Best Time of Year to Clean a Deck Before Staining
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Timing your cleaning project affects both how well the cleaner works and how reliably the deck dries before you need to stain it.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Late spring through early fall is the ideal window</strong> in most climates, with daytime temperatures between 50°F and 85°F. Most cleaner and brightener chemistries need moderate warmth to fully activate — cold water and cold air both slow the chemical reaction, requiring longer dwell times and sometimes a second application to get full results. Staining season generally follows a few days to a week behind cleaning season for the same reason: the wood needs warm, dry conditions to both clean effectively and dry out fully afterward.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Avoid cleaning during extended wet or humid stretches.</strong> Wood that can't dry out between the final rinse and the stain application defeats the purpose of cleaning in the first place — you'll end up staining over damp wood regardless of how thoroughly it was cleaned, and trapped moisture is one of the most common causes of early peeling.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Fall cleaning has one specific advantage:</strong> if you're not staining until the following spring, a fall cleaning removes the season's accumulated dirt, pollen, and biological growth before it has all winter to set in and become harder to remove. Just plan to give the deck a light re-clean before staining if several months pass between the two steps — dust and light grime will have resettled by then even if nothing serious has grown back.
                </p>

                <h2 id="matching-cleaner-to-wood-species" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Matching Cleaner to Wood Species
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Cleaner chemistry that's perfectly safe on one wood species can be too aggressive — or not aggressive enough — on another. A few species-specific notes worth knowing before you buy:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Pressure-treated pine</strong> tolerates oxygen bleach and mill-glaze-cutting cleaners well and is the most common candidate for the mill-glaze category above on new installs. Older PT decks that have weathered gray respond well to an oxalic brightener pass after cleaning.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Cedar and redwood</strong> are naturally more tannin-rich than pine, which means they're more prone to visible tannin bleed-through and blotchy graying — and they respond especially well to oxalic acid brightening, since these species tend to show UV weathering as a more pronounced gray-to-orange discoloration than pine does. Avoid overly aggressive scrubbing or high-pressure rinsing on cedar specifically; it's a softer wood and raises grain more readily than pine. See our <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-cedar/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">best deck stain for cedar</a> guide for finish recommendations once the wood is clean and bright.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Tropical hardwoods</strong> like ipe and cumaru are dense enough that standard cleaners often sit on the surface rather than penetrating — expect longer dwell times and more scrub effort, and always confirm readiness with a water-bead test rather than a fixed schedule, since these woods dry and absorb on a different timeline than softwoods entirely.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Composite decking</strong> (Trex, TimberTech, and similar products) is not wood and should never be cleaned with wood-specific brighteners or strippers — those products are formulated to chemically react with wood lignin and tannins that composite material doesn't have, and can discolor or damage the composite surface. Composite manufacturers sell dedicated cleaning products for their material; use those instead.
                </p>

                <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Frequently Asked Questions
                </h2>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I really need to clean my deck before staining if it looks fine?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. Deck surfaces accumulate a fine layer of pollen, oxidized wood fiber, and airborne grime that isn't always visible but is enough to interfere with stain adhesion. Even a deck that "looks fine" typically shows a visible difference in stain absorption on a cleaned versus uncleaned section — cleaning is cheap insurance against a stain job that fails early.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use the same cleaner for mold and for gray weathering?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Not effectively. Mold and mildew are biological growth that needs an oxidizing or mildewcide cleaner to kill at the root. Gray weathering is UV-damaged wood fiber that needs oxalic acid to chemically reverse. Many deck restoration projects need both steps in sequence — clean for biology and dirt first, rinse, then brighten for color — rather than expecting one product to do both jobs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is pressure washing enough on its own, without a chemical cleaner?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">No. A pressure washer alone removes surface dirt and can blast away visible mold spotting, but it doesn't kill mold roots embedded in the wood cells and does nothing to reverse UV graying. Used without a chemical cleaner first, high pressure also raises and damages wood grain, which can actually make stain absorption less even. Use a cleaner to do the chemical work, and pressure washing (at moderate PSI) as the rinse step, not a substitute for it.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my deck needs a stripper instead of a cleaner?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">If there's existing stain on the deck that's intact and simply faded, a cleaner (and brightener, if it's grayed) is sufficient — most semitransparent stains can be recoated after cleaning without stripping. If the existing stain is peeling, flaking, cracking, or you're changing from a solid or semi-solid stain to a different type, it needs to come off completely with a stripper first. Cleaning over failing stain just cleans around the problem instead of fixing it.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What PSI should I use to rinse a deck?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Keep pressure washers under roughly 1,200–1,500 PSI with a wide fan tip (25–40 degrees) for deck cleaning, held at a consistent 12–18 inch distance and moving steadily. Higher pressure, a narrow tip, or holding the wand too close raises and splinters wood fiber — a mistake that's difficult to fully undo and that leaves the surface absorbing stain unevenly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I skip brightening and just clean the deck?</h4>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">You can, but only if the wood isn't visibly gray after cleaning and drying. Cleaning removes dirt and biological growth; it doesn't reverse UV-caused graying because that's a change to the wood fiber itself, not a surface contaminant. Stain applied directly over gray, degraded fiber without brightening first tends to look dull and absorb unevenly, even though the wood is technically clean.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

