                <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Deck Cleaning Mistakes
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    Cleaning is the step that decides whether a stain lasts one season or four, and it is also the step most likely to damage the wood it is meant to prepare.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing Too Aggressively</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        A narrow tip held close will carve the soft spring wood out from between the growth rings, leaving a furry, fuzzed surface that drinks stain unevenly forever after. Use a wide fan tip, keep the wand moving, stay at least a foot back, and let the cleaning chemistry do the work rather than the pressure.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing a Cleaner With a Brightener</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        They are two different products doing two different jobs. Cleaners are alkaline and lift dirt, mildew, and greying; brighteners are acidic and neutralise the cleaner while restoring the wood tone and reopening the grain. Skipping the brightener leaves the surface alkaline, which dulls the colour and can interfere with stain adhesion.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Before the Deck Is Genuinely Dry</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Wood that feels dry on the surface can still be saturated a few millimetres down, and stain applied over it will not penetrate — it sits on top and peels. Give the deck at least 48 hours of dry weather after cleaning, and test by sprinkling water: if it soaks in rather than beading, the wood is ready.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting Cleaner Dry on the Surface</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Deck cleaners need to stay wet for their full dwell time and then be rinsed off completely. Allowed to dry in place, particularly in sun, they leave streaks and lap marks that show straight through the stain. Work in shaded sections, keep the surface wet, and rinse thoroughly.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Closing -->
                <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    The best <strong class="text-white">deck cleaner</strong> is the one matched to what is actually on your deck: a percarbonate or hypochlorite cleaner for mildew and grey, a dedicated stripper where old film-forming stain has to come off, and an oxalic acid brightener afterwards in every case.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    The sequence matters more than the brand. Clean, brighten, and then give the deck a full 48 dry hours before staining. That is what turns a stain job into one that lasts three or four seasons instead of coming back next spring.
                  </p>
                </div>

                <div class="mt-16 text-center bg-blue-50 p-12 rounded-3xl border border-blue-100 shadow-inner">
                    <h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                        Clean First. Stain Second. Skip Nothing.
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-8 max-w-xl mx-auto">
                        A weekend spent cleaning and brightening properly is the difference between a stain job that lasts three to four years and one that peels before the next holiday. Match the cleaner to your actual problem, run the water-bead test before you commit, and the rest of the project gets a lot more forgiving.
                    </p>
